    {
      "dom_id": "question-34605",
      "name": null,
      "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14236308/assets/d5da28420cf16c4563fbf7188a81b16c4cf8aaa62af39998456fe7016b76aa7d.png)\n\nWhat is the domain of the function $f(x),$ shown above?",
      "worked_solution": "The function $f(x)$ above is defined for values between $x=-1$ and $x=5.$\n\n- Since there is a closed circle at $x=5,$ this point is included in the domain.\n\n- On the other hand, because there is an open circle at $x=-1,$ this point is not included in the domain.\n\nSo, the function $f(x)$ above is defined for values between $x=-1$ and $x=5,$ including $x=5$ but not including $x=-1.$\n\nTherefore, the domain is\n\n$$\nx\\in (-1,5].\n$$\n\nNote that the function is defined at the point $x=3,$  even though the function jumps at that point. The function value at $x=3$ is $1.$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
